%[text] # LAB 2 | Build-Your-Own Planet!
%[text] ## Planetesimal Accretion Model
%[text] This exercise uses a simplified gravitational *n*-body simulation to simulate planetary accretion through planetesimal collisions and the compositional evolution of planetary embryos.
%[text] *N*-body simulations track the positions of a large number bodies of a set mass as they gravitationally interact with each other. It does so by applying Newton's laws of motion (here without relativistic corrections) which state that a body changes velocity as a function of the balance of forces it experiences. The force balance arises from totalling up the gravitational attraction a body feels from all other bodies surrounding it.
%[text] Planetesimals are initialised with random masses and positions within a disk extending to ~20 AU (Astronomical Unit = distance from Earth to Sun) from a massive central star. A gas giant can optionally be placed at the snow line 5 AU from the central star.
%[text] The model tracks three components—metal, rock, and ice—assuming a radial distribution: metal- and rock-rich in the inner disk, ice-dominated beyond the snow line at 5 AU.
%[text] Collisions occur when bodies pass within an effective collision radius. The colliding masses, compositions, and momenta merge into a single body, positioned at the centre of mass and moving with momentum-conserving velocity.
%[text] 
%[text] ## Task 1 – First Look
%[text] Begin by running the model with the default parameters set in the demo case. Click the **Run** button (green triangle) in the menu bar. To stop the simulation, click **Stop** (red square).
%[text] Output figures are saved in the `out/` folder of your MATLAB Drive or current working directory. Review the numbered frames to see how the accretion model evolves over time. What patterns do you notice?
%[text] Each time frame produces three figures:
%[text] - a **top view** of the protoplanetary disk,
%[text] - a **radial view** of the disk, and
%[text] - a **planetary zoo plot** of planetary radius vs. mass (relative to Earth). \
%[text] In the disk views, body sizes are scaled to radius, with colours showing composition (red = metal core, green = rocky mantle, blue = icy envelope). The Sun and a gas giant appear in black (not to scale). For orientation, Earth’s orbit and size are indicated. The five most massive bodies are highlighted in magenta and shown at 2× magnification in the radial views.
%[text] 
%[text] ## Task 2 – Parameter Testing
%[text] At the end of the script you will find several model parameters that can be adjusted within preset ranges. Explore these options and test a few variations. You only need to run each simulation briefly to see the effect of a new parameter set.
%[text] To save output figures separately for each test, assign a new run identifier each time. Use clear `Run IDs`, e.g. `NumberBodies500` for a run initialised with 500 bodies.
%[text] You can also change the `random seed`, which controls the initial random distribution of planetesimals. Adjusting this number produces unique outcomes for otherwise identical parameter sets.
%[text] 
%[text] ## Task 3 –  Number and Mass of Planetesimals
%[text] Run three simulations with varying initial numbers and masses of planetesimals:
%[text] - 1000 bodies, average mass 0.01
%[text] - 300 bodies, average mass 0.03
%[text] - 2000 bodies, average mass 0.005 \
%[text] Set the stopping time to at least 100 years and increase the output interval to 300 or more to avoid excessive figures.
%[text] In each case, the total mass is similar, but distributed across different numbers of bodies. What differences do you observe in the simulation outcomes?
%[text] 
%[text] ## Task 4 – Effects of a Gas Giant
%[text] The model includes a **gas giant** orbiting at 5 AU. Gas giants are thought to form early in protoplanetary disks, when icy particles and gas are abundant beyond the snow line. Their large mass strongly influences nearby orbits and the formation of later planets.
%[text] Run three simulations with gas giant masses of 10, 100, and 1000 Earth masses. Assign a new `Run ID` for each case, and run to at least 100 years with the output interval set to 300 or higher.
%[text] Which case corresponds roughly to Jupiter’s mass in our solar system? What differences do you observe as the gas giant becomes more massive?
%[text] 
%[text] ## Task 5 – Final Simulation
%[text] The model artificially increases collision likelihood so that planets form within a manageable runtime. For this last test, increase the collision detection distance to 0.1 and run the model with as many bodies, and for as long, as time (and patience) allow.
%[text] At the final state, describe the five most massive bodies formed. Do they resemble any planets in our solar system? Comparing their mass and radius (relative to Earth’s radius shown on the plots) with the exoplanetary zoo figure, where would they fall? Terrestrial planet, super-Earth, water world, or ice giant?
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:4676)
%[text] **Figure:** Exoplanetary Zoo after Zeng et al., *PNAS*, 2019 showing radius vs. mass of confirmed exoplanet discoveries. Data shown relative to Earth, with colour indicating estimated surface temperature and some trends indicating typical compositional categories. 
%%

%%
%[text] # 
%[text] # Set Model Parameters
%%
clear; close all; clc;  % clear workspace
%%
%[text] Set run identifier tag. Output figures will be saved to folder `out/runID`. Set new identifier for every model run.
%%
runID = 'N1e3_Ggt300';  % change RunID for every parameter test %[control:editfield:9b4c]{"position":[9,22]}
%%
%[text] Set seed for random number generator. Adjust the slider to your favourite number to produce uniquely randomised results.
%%
seed =15;  % change the random seed %[control:slider:8103]{"position":[7,9]}
%%
%[text] Set interval of time elapsed in \[yr\] between plotting figures and saving output. Select lower interval for testing, higher for full model run.
%%
nop  =  100;  % select lower interval for testing, higher interval for full run %[control:dropdown:6d55]{"position":[9,12]}
%%
%[text] Set model stopping time in years. Select shorter stopping time for testing, higher for full run. 
%%
tend  =  100000;  % select shorter stopping time for testing, higher for full run %[control:dropdown:123d]{"position":[10,16]}
%%
%[text] Set initial condition for *n-*body simulation. Select the number of planetesimals, their mean mass, the mass of the gas giant planted at 5 AU, and the mass of the central star \[Earth Masses\], and the initial mean inclination ("vertical" position out of disk). 
%%
N     = 1000;  % initial number of bodies %[control:dropdown:8195]{"position":[9,13]}
MPls  = 0.01;  % mean planetesimal mass [Earth Masses] %[control:dropdown:3d84]{"position":[9,13]}
MGgt  = 300;  % mass of gas giant [Earth Masses] %[control:dropdown:7780]{"position":[9,12]}
MStr  = 3e5;  % mass of central star [Earth Masses] %[control:dropdown:985b]{"position":[9,12]}
Incl  = 2;  % initial mean inclination [AU] %[control:dropdown:0e0f]{"position":[9,10]}
%%
%[text] Set effective collision radius: the radial distance \[AU\] to which nearby bodies are merged as collisions.
%%
cls   = 0.01;  % collision radius [AU] %[control:dropdown:64a3]{"position":[9,13]}
%[text] **Click on the button below or the Run button in the menu bar to start the model.**
  %[control:button:0ba5]{"position":[1,2]}

restart = 0;
run('../src/main');

